    watchText: 'Memorising "the Twitter design" or "the Uber design" from a video fails the moment the interviewer changes one constraint.',
    tags: ['Asked from SDE-2 onward', 'Rewards trade-off thinking', 'Weak without DSA first'],
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    badge: 'The fastest resume-line ROI',
    title: 'One cloud platform: AWS or Azure',
    body:
      'Almost nothing you build in college gets deployed anywhere real without touching a cloud platform. A foundational cloud certification is inexpensive, takes a few weeks of focused study, and signals you understand how software actually runs in production — not just on your laptop.',
    fitText: 'Anyone who has finished at least one working project and wants it to look deployable, not just coded.',
    watchText: 'Collecting the badge without ever deploying a real project on that cloud defeats the point of taking it.',
    tags: ['Low cost, short timeline', 'Works for AWS or Azure', 'Backed by a real deployed project'],
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    badge: 'Depth beats a scattered toolbox',
    title: 'One language mastered, plus real CS fundamentals',
    body:
      'Most CS syllabi teach a language shallowly across many subjects instead of one language deeply. Pick one language you will actually build in — Python, Java, or JavaScript, depending on your target track — and go deep enough to reason about memory, concurrency, and performance, not just syntax.',
    fitText: 'First and second-year students still deciding what to specialise in.',
    watchText: 'Jumping between five languages in one semester because a video told you to "future-proof" your resume rarely builds real depth in any of them.',
    tags: ['Depth over breadth', 'Feeds directly into DSA practice', 'Free official docs are often enough'],
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    badge: 'The fastest-growing lane right now',
    title: 'AI and machine learning fundamentals',
    body:
      'You do not need to become a research scientist to benefit from this. A foundational ML course teaches you how models are trained, evaluated, and where they break — which matters even if your actual job ends up being backend, product, or data engineering next to an AI feature.',
    fitText: 'Third and final-year students, and anyone targeting product companies building AI features.',
    watchText: 'Collecting AI course certificates without ever training or evaluating a real model on real data teaches vocabulary, not skill.',
    tags: ['Highest current demand growth', 'Math-light entry courses exist', 'Needs a real project to count'],
  },
  {
    num: '06',
    badge: 'Only if this is genuinely your route',
    title: 'GATE-aligned CS fundamentals (NPTEL)',
    body:
      'If you are aiming at an MTech seat, a PSU technical role, or a teaching/research path, the theory GATE tests — operating systems, DBMS, computer networks, theory of computation — is worth revisiting properly through free IIT-faculty lectures, not last-minute cramming.',
    fitText: 'Students who genuinely want MTech, a PSU technical role, or academia — not everyone.',
    watchText: 'Preparing for GATE "just in case" while ignoring DSA and projects for three years is a common, expensive mistake.',
    tags: ['Free, IIT-faculty taught', 'Only relevant for specific paths', 'Deep on core CS theory'],
