const stackTable = [
  {
    area: 'DSA',
    free: 'Striver\'s A2Z DSA Sheet (takeUforward, Raj Vikramaditya) — free problem list with video explanations; Love Babbar\'s 450 DSA Sheet; Apna College and Kunal Kushwaha\'s free YouTube DSA playlists',
    paid: 'takeUforward\'s TUF+ paid track for structured contests and mentorship, if you want built-in accountability',
    output: 'A public GitHub with your solved problems, organised by pattern, not just a "150/450 solved" screenshot',
  },
  {
    area: 'System design',
    free: 'ByteByteGo\'s free newsletter and YouTube breakdowns; Gaurav Sen\'s system design YouTube series',
    paid: 'Grokking the System Design Interview (originally on Educative, now also on DesignGurus.io)',
    output: 'One written design doc for a real system (a URL shortener, a chat app) with the trade-offs you chose and why',
  },
  {
    area: 'Cloud',
    free: 'AWS Free Tier plus AWS Skill Builder\'s free foundational content; Microsoft Learn\'s free AZ-900 modules',
    paid: 'The official exam fee for AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner (CLF-C02) or Microsoft Azure Fundamentals (AZ-900)',
    output: 'One personal or college project actually deployed and reachable on that cloud, not just a certificate PDF',
  },
  {
    area: 'Core language + CS fundamentals',
    free: 'CS50: Introduction to Computer Science (Harvard, free on edX and via cs50.harvard.edu) for fundamentals; official language documentation (Python docs, MDN for JavaScript)',
    paid: 'Rarely needed here — the free official material is usually enough if you actually do the problem sets',
    output: 'One project built without a tutorial open beside you, even if it is small',
  },
  {
    area: 'AI / ML',
    free: 'Google\'s free "Machine Learning Crash Course"; fast.ai\'s free practical deep-learning course',
    paid: 'Machine Learning Specialization by Andrew Ng (Coursera, DeepLearning.AI and Stanford Online)',
    output: 'One small trained model with a short write-up of the data, the accuracy, and exactly where it fails',
  },
  {
    area: 'GATE-aligned theory (if relevant)',
    free: 'NPTEL courses taught by IIT faculty on operating systems, DBMS, computer networks, and theory of computation',
    paid: 'A GATE-specific coaching subscription, only if self-study alone is not sticking after an honest attempt',
    output: 'A working score in full-length mock tests, not just "watched the lecture"',
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Fit',
    body: 'Is this course teaching a skill you will actually use in the track you are aiming at, or does it just sound impressive on a resume?',
    note: 'A cloud certification means little if you never plan to deploy anything.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body: 'Does the cost and time fit your current semester, your money, and your family\'s realistic education budget?',
    note: 'A ₹15,000 paid bootcamp is not automatically better than a free structured sheet plus discipline.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body: 'Is this skill actually showing up in the internship and job listings you can see right now, not just in a course marketing page?',
    note: 'Check real listings on LinkedIn and Naukri for your target role before committing months to a course.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival',
    body: 'Does this course teach judgement AI tools cannot yet supply — trade-offs, debugging, architecture decisions — or does it teach something an AI assistant can already do for you?',
    note: 'DSA, system design, and applied ML all pass this test. Memorising syntax mostly does not, anymore.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Collecting five certificates instead of building one real skill',
    body: 'A stack of Coursera certificates with no project behind any of them is not a skill portfolio. It is proof you can finish a course, which is a much weaker signal than proof you can build something.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Skipping DSA because "I want to do AI, not competitive coding"',
    body: 'Almost every hiring loop for software and ML-adjacent roles still opens with a DSA round. Skipping it does not remove the round — it just means you show up unprepared for it.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Paying for an expensive bootcamp before testing the free version of the same skill',
    body: 'Sample the subject through a free sheet, a free YouTube series, or a free platform tier first. Pay only when structure, mentorship, or accountability is the actual thing missing — not the information itself.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Treating your degree branch as your whole identity',
    body: 'A B.Tech CSE, a BCA, and a B.Sc CS graduate with the same GitHub, the same DSA depth, and the same shipped projects compete on close to equal footing for most software roles. The degree opens some doors; the skill stack decides what happens once you are inside them.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Choosing every course based on what is trending this month',
    body: 'AI is genuinely growing fast, but chasing whatever is trending without a real DSA and systems foundation underneath it produces a shallow profile that struggles the moment an interviewer asks "why," not just "what."',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What are the best courses for computer science and CSE students right now?',
    a: 'It depends which question you are actually asking. If you are choosing your undergraduate path after 12th, compare B.Tech CSE, BCA, and B.Sc CS on entry exams, cost, and career doors. If you are already enrolled in a CS/CSE degree, the highest-value add-ons are DSA (Striver\'s A2Z Sheet or Love Babbar\'s sheet), one cloud certification (AWS Cloud Practitioner or Azure AZ-900), a system design course once you have DSA down, and a foundational AI/ML course such as Andrew Ng\'s Machine Learning Specialization.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I choose B.Tech CSE, BCA, or B.Sc Computer Science after 12th?',
    a: 'B.Tech CSE is the widest-recognised route and the only one that opens core-engineering and most PSU technical doors, but it needs JEE Main, JEE Advanced, or a state CET. BCA is faster and cheaper, and works well if your goal is software or web development specifically. B.Sc CS suits students who want a lighter fee, more theory and statistics depth, and are open to a master\'s (MSc or MCA) afterward. For most private-sector software roles, a strong DSA and project portfolio narrows the gap between these three faster than the degree label does.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I really need to learn DSA if I am not targeting a FAANG-style company?',
    a: 'Yes, in almost every case. DSA rounds are not limited to elite product companies anymore — most product companies, many well-funded startups, and a growing number of service companies now test basic problem-solving ability in interviews. Treat DSA as the entry-level skill floor for software roles generally, not a niche prep track for a handful of elite employers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a paid system design course worth it, or is free content enough?',
    a: 'Start free — ByteByteGo\'s newsletter and YouTube content, and Gaurav Sen\'s system design series, cover the core concepts well. Pay for a structured course like Grokking the System Design Interview only once you already understand DSA and want a more organised, practice-heavy path with mock scenarios, rather than paying before you have tested the free material.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I get an AWS or an Azure certification first?',
    a: 'Either is a reasonable starting point, and the honest answer is that the specific cloud matters less than actually deploying something on it. AWS has the larger market share in India\'s hiring listings overall, so AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner is a slightly safer default if you have no other signal pulling you toward Azure. If your target companies or internship already uses Azure, AZ-900 is the better first move.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a non-CS student still get into computer science careers using these courses?',
    a: 'Yes, for several tracks — general software development, cloud, and cybersecurity in particular are more degree-agnostic than people assume, and a strong project portfolio can substitute for a weaker-brand or non-CS degree. AI/ML research-heavy roles and government or PSU technology posts still lean much more heavily on a formal CS or engineering credential, so weigh the specific track before assuming self-study alone is enough.',
  },
